[{:seon.task/name "durability-is-continuous-group-commit"
  :seon.task/plan "Laws audit #6 (notes/AUDIT-2026-10-21-laws.md). Durability is decided by a clock: `seon.writer/flusher-proc` sleeps out the rest of `seon.db/flush-ms` (10 ms, a literal since 9b969cc removed its config key and kept it as a \"guard\") after the writer's first dirty notice, then asks the writer for {:op :flush}; and `seon.agent/transact!` commits the agent's run-ahead writes when 10 ms have passed since its last commit. The owner's ruling (notes/DECISIONS.md, \"Durability is batched everywhere\", the paragraph superseded 2026-10-21) is continuous group commit with no window: a write is answered when committed in memory; the flusher parks until something is unflushed, syncs once for everything committed so far, answers the waiters it covers and loops; commits arriving during a sync share the next one, so the batch follows the load and an idle system never syncs; a :flush? write (one an outside effect follows) waits for the sync that covers it. v1 had no flusher: every recording was one synchronous transaction, durable before its reply (~/src/seon CLAUDE.md, \"Recover facts, not execution\"); mg/check's alternative, the writer syncing inline after each batch, is not taken because a Flow step's answers leave only after its transform returns, so every answer would wait for the fsync, against the ruling. Correct: (1) `seon.writer/writer-proc` answers a batch unflushed and, when the store is dirty and no notice is outstanding, sends the flusher {:op :dirty :commits n :since ns}, n its commit count (`:commits` in its state, :agent-commit notices counted too) and :since a measurement only; (2) `seon.writer/flusher-proc` takes a notice and syncs at once (`seon.db/sync!` on its :cfg, the writer's store config, a binding in resources/seon/system.edn), with every notice queued meanwhile covered by that one sync, counts :flushes, and answers the writer {:op :flushed :commits n}; (3) the writer, on {:op :flushed :commits n}: clean (dirty false, notice no longer outstanding, :flushes counted) when n is its commit count, else still dirty and it notifies again with its current count; :flush? requests keep syncing inline before their answer; (4) `seon.agent/transact!` commits at idle and at turn end only (and before any wait, task `seon.agent/writes-commit-before-any-wait`), never because time passed. Verify first, at the REPL: LMDB's env sync (`seon.db/sync!`, MDB_NOSYNC) from the flusher's thread while the writer commits is safe (mdb_env_sync is documented as callable concurrently with write transactions); if not, that is a failure-points row and the plan returns to the planner. Deletes `seon.db/flush-ms`, the flusher's Thread/sleep and :flush-ms param, :flush-ms in `seon.root/start!`'s options and bindings and in resources/seon/system.edn, the writer's {:op :flush} request, and the clock in `seon.agent/transact!`; seon.check's flush-window check (`:flush-ms 1000`) becomes a check that an answered unflushed write survives only to the last sync. Estimate: per batch, one notice; per sync, one env sync and one answer; an idle system: no step at all."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/the-flush-is-continuous-group-commit}}]
